# ruff: noqa: E501  (SQL strings, rule tables)
"""P10: map MusicBrainz tags to 12 top-level genres and give every analysis unit one genre.

**Tag map** (``data/reference/genre_map.csv``, regenerated by ``--write-map`` and committed).
Every MusicBrainz official genre, plus a short allow-list of frequent non-genre tags with an
unambiguous meaning (``soundtrack``, ``vgm``, ``hip-hop``, ``pop/rock`` …), is mapped by
``map_tag``: exact overrides first, then ordered regex rules, most specific genre first, so
``pop punk`` -> punk, ``rap metal`` -> metal, ``folk rock`` -> folk/country, ``blues rock`` ->
rock. Unmatched official genres fall to ``other`` (rule ``default``). The CSV records the rule
that fired, the MB parent genre(s) where the export has them, and each tag's weight in the main
sample, so the high-weight rows can be reviewed by hand.

**Assignment.** For one entity, each mapped tag adds its (positive) vote count to its genre; the
genre with the most votes wins, ties going to the earlier genre in ``GENRES`` (specific before
generic, ``other`` last). ``genre_share`` is the winner's share of mapped votes. Sources, in
order; a unit takes the first one with any mapped tag (``genre_source``):

1. ``rg``        release-group tags (the brief's source)
2. ``release``   release tags
3. ``recording`` recording tags
4. ``artist``    tags of the release group's credited artist(s), summed over artists
5. ``hl``        AcousticBrainz high-level classifier (fallback only, flagged): the most
                 common label over the unit's submissions, mapped to the 12 genres
6. none          ``genre`` is NULL

**Reference mix (P19 targets).** Every MusicBrainz Album/EP/Single release group with a first-
release year gets a genre from sources 1 and 4 (the ones defined for all of MB). Shares by year
are written to ``data/processed/genre_mix.parquet`` alongside the AcousticBrainz main-sample
shares (per unit and per release group) and the implied reweighting factor.

    uv run python -m lw.build.genres --write-map   # regenerate the reviewed map (rare)
    uv run python -m lw.build.genres               # assign genres, add them to tracks.parquet
"""

